From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 15:36:49 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10928/11362] Record that the verdict guards reject honest
 partial self-classification

The V608 capstone burned three attempts and retired, every one logged
artifact_not_updated_past_bootstrap. The artifact was written each time, is
clean, and says partial_v608_capstone_contract_audited_science_incomplete, which
is true -- ten of twelve tasks had been cascade-blocked. It is the second
capstone lost this way today; exp6922 went the same route this morning.

Measured against _verdict_is_untrustworthy, the bare partial_ prefix is
rejected, a terminal prefix paired with verdict_class partial is rejected, and
the identical verdict string with the verdict_class field simply removed is
accepted. The only accepted route is to omit the self-classification.

That is an incentive pointing the wrong way at the moment it matters most. A
milestone went badly, the capstone reported it accurately, and the loop
responded by burning three attempts and retiring the task. An agent optimising
to get its work recorded learns to drop verdict_class. Nobody wrote that rule;
two guards imply it between them.

Both guards are individually defensible, which is why it survived. The
terminal-prefix rule exists because verdicts containing partial or blocked
caused false-positive retries across five prior incidents. The verdict_class
check exists so a complete_ prefix cannot mask a partial result. Neither
anticipated an artifact that is finished, honest and partial at once, which is
what a capstone over a blocked milestone has to be.

Not fixed: verdict classification governs what the loop re-runs across every
task type, so it is an operator call. Records the fix shape and an interim
instruction that explicitly refuses the obvious workaround, since deleting
verdict_class is the guard teaching an agent to hide a finding.

